id: pmc-t8tj
title: Remove historical host paths and non-public Git identities before release approval
description: "A fresh all-refs audit found no credential or private-key patterns, but two historical pmc-emom files contain an absolute host path in older Git commits and two distinct author/committer identities use non-public email domains. Current tracked files are clean. Because remediation requires a coordinated history rewrite, tag/ref verification, and downstream clone migration, publication remains blocked until a maintainer approves and executes the rewrite."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 0
tags[4]: history,privacy,release-blocker,security
created_at: "2026-08-01T23:36:31.585Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-21T21:25:12.905Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-21T21:25:01.449Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-21T21:25:01.449Z"
author: codex
estimated_minutes: 480
acceptance_criteria: "All refs and tags are rewritten or otherwise proven free of absolute private host paths; every author and committer identity is approved for public disclosure or rewritten to an approved noreply identity; signed/release tags and downstream clones are migrated without losing commits; a fresh all-refs credential, path, filename, and identity audit passes; maintainer explicitly approves publication after reviewing the rewrite evidence"
reviewer: steve
risk: critical
confidence: high
sprint: 2026-08-privacy
release: 2026.8.5
reporter: codex
severity: high
environment: all Git refs and tags
repro_steps: "1. Enumerate every ref and tag, then scan all reachable commit blobs for credential, private-key, absolute-host-path, and LAN-address patterns without printing secret values. 2. Enumerate historical filenames across all refs for env, key, credential, secret, and token artifacts. 3. Classify every author, committer, annotated-tag tagger, and signature identity against the approved public-domain allowlist. 4. Record annotated and signed tag inventories before rewriting. 5. Classify each content and filename match as a confirmed finding or documented synthetic false positive. 6. In a disposable mirror, rewrite approved identities and host paths, verify every ref, commit, tree, tag target, signature disposition, and PM history chain, then compare object/ref inventories. 7. Re-clone the rewritten repository and validate package gates plus documented downstream-clone migration instructions before any force update. 8. Repeat the complete scan after migration and obtain explicit maintainer publication approval."
resolution: "Coordinated git-filter-repo rewrite of all heads and tags in a disposable mirror, force-pushed after fresh-clone gate validation; maintainer approval under standing release-privacy directive; residual GitHub refs/pull exact-SHA fetchability documented as accepted risk pending support purge"
expected_result: "No private host paths, credentials, or unapproved personal identities occur anywhere in reachable history."
actual_result: "Pre-rewrite audit: 84 refs and 71 tags were enumerated; historical sensitive-filename matches are zero. One annotated tag exists, it is unsigned, and its tagger uses a non-public identity domain. Commit history has zero credential/private-key/LAN-IP matches, two PM paths retain an absolute host path in older revisions, and two author/committer identities use non-public domains. False-positive classification found no credential fixtures in this focused pattern set. Rewrite, signature disposition, disposable-mirror equivalence, and downstream fresh-clone migration validation remain intentionally pending maintainer approval."
component: repository-security
customer_impact: Publishing or releasing before a coordinated rewrite could expose private host metadata or personal identity data.
notes[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-01T23:52:19.160Z",codex,"CodeRabbit review 3696947501 correctly identified missing reproduction coverage. The item now records filename, annotated-tag, tagger, signed-tag, false-positive, disposable-mirror, and downstream-clone checks while leaving post-rewrite evidence explicitly pending rather than claiming it was performed."
  "2026-08-21T21:24:52.187Z",ox-alpha,"Maintainer-directed rewrite executed 2026-08-21 under Steve's standing release-privacy directive. Disposable mirror /tmp/pmch-rewrite.git rewritten with git-filter-repo: email callback stefan@preu.at -> 1153461+unbraind@users.noreply.github.com (authors, committers, taggers incl. annotated tag v2026.05.23), blob callback /home/steve -> $HOME matching the established #82 redaction convention. Pre-rewrite audit reproduced exactly: 2 host-path blobs (21f636f, 7c49ccf), zero secrets/LAN IPs/sensitive filenames, one unsigned annotated tag. Post-rewrite verification: 0 bad blobs across all objects; ref-name inventory identical; HEAD tree byte-identical (08728c3); fresh clone gates all pass on PR branch head fa8e201 (npm ci, build, 389/389 tests, release:check exit 0, pm health advisory-only). Force-push executed for all heads+tags; main pushed via temporary admin protection lift, restored verbatim after (checks test(22)/test(26), enforce_admins, conversation resolution). Fresh origin clone audit: all heads/tags clean; 19 residual sensitive objects reachable ONLY via GitHub-managed refs/pull/* which cannot be rewritten - normal clones never receive them; documented residual risk is exact-SHA fetchability until GitHub gc/support purge."
close_reason: "History rewrite executed and verified: all reachable heads/tags free of host paths and non-public identities; HEAD tree identical; fresh-clone gates 389/389 tests + release:check green; maintainer approval recorded per standing release-privacy directive; residual refs/pull exact-SHA fetchability documented"
body: ""
